# Volatility decomposition

Things that change per church or template (who can add, parent required, labels, deepest vs mid-layer) must not live in the same place as things that stay stable (wizard steps, `POST /api/structure/nodes`).

## Managers first

A **manager** is a pure function (or small module) that answers policy from domain data. No JSX, no `fetch`, no `standardType === 'Fellowship'` / `'Cell'` string gates.

Before adding a branch in a view or wizard:

1. Look for an existing manager (`createUnitPolicy`, helpers in `structure-tree.ts`, abilities).
2. Reuse or extend it. `createUnitPolicy` is the create-unit source of truth — Units list, drill-in, and later giving/attendance placement should consume it, not copy its `if`s.
3. Only add a new manager when the question is new (e.g. “can this member sit here?”), not when the screen is new.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — policy baked into the screen
if (layer.standardType === 'Fellowship') openFellowshipWizard()
else openNamePopover()

// ✅ GOOD — one engine, policy from the manager
const policy = createUnitPolicy(tree, layer, scopeUnitId)
<AddButton disabled={!policy.canAdd} title={policy.blockedReason} />
<UnitCreateWizard ... />
```

## Engines second

An **engine** renders or persists. It reads the manager and stays layer-agnostic. Labels come from `displayName` / policy labels, not hardcoded “Fellowship” or “Cell”.

## When a feature spans churches

If Church → Cell and Fellowship → Cell would need different `if`s, that is a missing manager, not two components. Lock the manager with unit tests for at least two template shapes before wiring UI.

## Teach while decomposing

Do not silently extract. For each split, say out loud: what changes (policy), what stays (engine), and what would break if we had left the `if` in the screen. Walk the manager so the human can explain it back. Prefer one concrete example from this repo (`createUnitPolicy`) plus one reading (Sandi Metz, [The Wrong Abstraction](https://sandimetz.com/blog/2016/1/20/the-wrong-abstraction)).
